! C22-6578 PROBLEM 1.8, "SIMULATION OF RENAME INSTRUCTION", printed
! p.10 (PDF 11), read in the ink.
!
!   "Create the effect of the instruction RNX, $1, DOG ($3).  Do not
!   simulate the indicator settings."
!
! The nine printed statements below are exact.  What the deck adds is
! the set-up before RNAME and the data.
!
! THE EFFECT BEING SIMULATED is Reference Manual printed p.27, Rename:
! "The index word in the index register specified by the field J is
! stored in the location addressed by the refill field of X0.  The
! refill field of X0 is then replaced by bits 0-17 of the effective
! address.  The remaining bits of X0 remain unchanged.  Next, the index
! word specified by J is replaced by the word in core storage addressed
! by the new refill field of X0."
!
! So with $0's refill = OLDNAM, $1 = X1V, $3 = 2.0, and DOG + 2.0 = NEW:
!
!   old_name   OLDNAM holds $1's old contents
!   x0         $0 unchanged but for the refill field = DOG + 2.0
!   x1         $1 = NEW
!   x2         $2 = what it was: "save" / "restore"
!   x3         $3 = what it was
!
! THE COMMENT IS THE INTERESTING PART, and it is why $2 is used at all:
! "It would seem that the SR instruction could be altered such that
! the refill field of $0 is stored directly into the address field of
! STOX, and the use of $2 would be avoided.  This is not possible
! because in the SR operation of the refill field concerned is right
! appended by zero bits to create a 25 bit value field.  The latter is
! then stored.  The STOX instruction would be seriously altered if a
! direct SR operation is used."
!
! THAT IS CHECKED DIRECTLY: printed "SR, $0, 18.0" writes the word that
! IS $2.  x2_during, an SX of $2 immediately after that SR, must show
! bits 0-17 = OLDNAM and bits 18-24 = 0, and bits 25-63 of $2 untouched.
!
! "LR, $0, 17.0" reads the value field of $1, which the LVE has just
! made the effective address: Reference Manual printed p.24, LR takes
! "bits 0-17 of the half-word specified".
!
! WHAT THE STATEMENTS DEPEND ON, by page: SR p.24; SX p.24; LVE p.29
! ("LVE, $1, LOX" loads $1's value with the effective address of the
! instruction at LOX, without executing it).
!
! THE SX AT x2_during IS NOT PRINTED, and it is the deck's only
! insertion between the printed statements.  It adds a half word and
! touches nothing the program reads.

        SLC, 1000.0
START   LX, $0, X0INIT
        LX, $1, X1INIT
        LX, $2, X2INIT
        LX, $3, X3INIT

RNAME   SX, $2, X2
        SR, $0, 18.0
        SX, $2, DURING
STOX    SX, $1, 0($2)
        LX, $2, X2
        LVE, $1, LOX
        LR, $0, 17.0
LOX     LX, $1, DOG($3)
HALT    BEW, $
X2      XW, 0

        SLC, 1100.0
X0INIT  XW, 3.0, 7, OLDNAM
X1INIT  XW, 5.0, 11, 1234
X2INIT  XW, 7.0, 13, 2345
X3INIT  XW, 2.0, 17, 3456
DURING  DD(BU,64,8), 0
OLDNAM  DD(BU,64,8), 0
DOG     DD(BU,64,8), 1
        DD(BU,64,8), 2
NEW     XW, 9.0, 19, 4567
        DD(BU,64,8), 4

        END, START
